WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:07.700
Your one percent Treaty is a piece of paper that says every signatory nation redirects one percent of its annual military

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:14.900
budget to enable any patient to participate in pragmatic clinical trials for the most promising new treatments.

3
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:23.420
The Math. The Problem: The world spends two point seven two trillion dollars a year on military forces.

4
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:32.150
The Hack: If every nation reduces military spending by one percent at the same time, the balance of power remains identical.

5
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:41.300
No country becomes more vulnerable. The Prize: That one percent, a twenty-seven point two billion dollar a year river of

6
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.120
cash, is redirected to fund clinical trials for every human disease.

7
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:54.460
For comparison, twenty-seven point two billion dollars is about what Americans spend on Valentine's Day (flowers and chocolate

8
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.620
over curing cancer, apparently), and roughly what humans spend on pet food every three months.

9
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.239
Apparently too much to ask for curing diseases. How It Works.

10
00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:10.700
Funding: Creates a twenty-seven point two billion dollar per year revenue stream for the one percent Treaty Fund.

11
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:21.140
Execution: A network of Decentralized Institutes of Health subsidizes patient participation in a Decentralized F.D.A., clinical

12
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.390
trials that are forty-four point one times cheaper than the current model.

13
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:36.060
Governance: Decentralized Autonomous Organization (D.A.O.) governed by smart contracts and global population via Wishocracy.

14
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.100
Transparency: All research, data, and spending published on public ledger.

15
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:49.820
This Isn't a Trade-Off. It's like if everyone in your neighborhood spent their life savings on attack dogs, and then called

16
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.180
it a "safe community" because all the dogs are equally vicious.

17
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.150
When every nation builds one percent fewer explosion devices at the same time, you get two good things: You become safer.

18
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.720
The global "you might all die by accident" temperature drops one percent.

19
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.699
Fewer chances of someone pressing the wrong button, which happens more than you'd think.

20
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:17.460
You stop dying from stupid things. That twenty-seven point two billion dollars pays sick people to try treatments instead

21
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:24.680
of waiting for death. Economists call this a Kaldor-Hicks improvement, which is their way of saying "the winners win more

22
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:31.660
than the losers lose, and we've decided that counts." In practice, some defense contractors lose one percent of their contracts.

23
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.680
In exchange, they and everyone they love become less likely to die of cancer.

24
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.700
Most people consider this a good trade. Defense contractors are still doing the math.

25
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.580
The Dead Capital Problem (Or: Why Bombs Are the Worst Investment Since Tulips).

26
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:54.900
Here's something nobody tells you: when countries "invest" in military hardware, they're not buying assets.

27
00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.140
They're buying very expensive paperweights that occasionally explode.

28
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:05.680
A bridge. Generates economic activity daily (trucks move, people commute, value flows).

29
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:11.100
Lasts fifty to one hundred years. Makes everyone richer. Pretty to look at.

30
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:17.890
A missile. Sits in a hole doing nothing. Costs one point five million dollars per year just to keep it from rotting.

31
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.440
Can only destroy things (including itself). Makes everyone nervous.

32
00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:29.040
This is what economists call "dead capital," resources trapped in a form that can't generate returns.

33
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.040
It's like if you converted your house into a pile of TNT. Sure, it's impressive.

34
00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:40.240
But you can't live in it, rent it out, or do anything except eventually blow it up, at which point you have neither a house

35
00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:48.200
nor TNT. As President Eisenhower (a five-star General who knew about killing professionally) put it: "Every gun that is

36
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:56.740
made, every warship launched, every rocket fired signifies, in the final sense, a theft from those who hunger and are not

37
00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:04.020
fed, those who are cold and are not clothed." He literally ran the biggest military in history, and even HE thought you

38
00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:10.160
were being ridiculous about this. A one percent treaty doesn't ask you to spend twenty-seven point two billion dollars curing

39
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.720
diseases. It asks you to redirect twenty-seven point two billion dollars from the least useful one percent of your military

40
00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:21.040
budget to things that make you richer and less dead. It's not spending.

41
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:29.420
It's stopping wasting. The Blueprint for a Saner World. Treaties are magic pieces of paper that countries sign when they

42
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:34.340
want to pretend they'll behave. Sometimes they work (you haven't had World War Three yet).

43
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.500
Sometimes they don't (you've had about two hundred other wars).

44
00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.980
But they're the only tool you have for getting countries to do anything together besides kill each other.

45
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:49.740
What Each Country Pays. Every signatory nation contributes exactly one percent of their military budget to the one percent

46
00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:56.900
Treaty Fund. U.S.A.: eight point eight six billion dollars (won't even notice, they've failed to account for over two point

47
